The user wants me to:
1. Extract the root view into its own class.
2. Extract the count label and increment button into its own view component.

Currently the root view is built in `SessionApp.rootView()` as a private method. Let me look at the current structure.

The `SessionApp.rootView()`:
```java
private View rootView() {
    return View.vstack(
            View.hstack(
                    View.text(countLabel),
                    View.button("Increment", () -> count.update(v -> v + 1))
            ).padding(16),
            View.textField("Your name", name),
            View.text(nameLabel),
            View.text("Pathland · Quarkus · per-session · 16-byte deltas")
                    .color(Color.rgb(0x88, 0x88, 0x88))
    ).padding(24);
}
```

The user wants:
1. The root view extracted into its own class (e.g., `CounterView`).
2. The count label + increment button (the hstack) extracted into its own view component (e.g., `CounterControls` or `CounterStepper`).

Now, there's a design consideration: the view references the signals (`count`, `countLabel`, `name`, `nameLabel`). If I extract these into classes, they need to accept the signals/bindings somehow.

Let me design:

**`CounterView`** — the root view class:
```java
public final class CounterView implements View {
    private final View counterControls;  // the extracted counter component
    private final View nameField;
    private final View nameLabel;
    private final View footer;

    public CounterView(WritableSignal<Integer> count, WritableSignal<String> name) {
        this.counterControls = new CounterControls(count);
        this.nameField = View.textField("Your name", name);
        this.nameLabel = View.text(name.map(n -> "Name: " + n));  // hmm
        ...
    }

    @Override
    public PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
        return View.vstack(
            counterControls,
            nameField,
            nameLabel,
            footer
        ).padding(24).render(env);
    }
}
```

Wait, but `View` is a sealed interface. The extracted classes must either implement `View` directly (and be added to the `permits` list!) or compose existing views.

The sealed interface `View` permits: Text, Image, Color, Rectangle, VStack, HStack, ZStack, Spacer, Button, TextField, Modified, ButtonStyleView, TapGestureView.

If I create new classes `CounterView` and `CounterControls` that `implements View`, I MUST add them to the `permits` list of the sealed interface. That's a constraint. The task said "sealed interface View" — adding new implementations to permits is fine (it's the mechanism for extending the DSL with custom views).

Alternatively, these could be factory methods / helper classes that BUILD and return a `View` (not implement it). E.g., `CounterView` is a static factory that returns a composed View. But the user said "Extract the root view into its own class" and "Extract the count label and button for increment to its own view component". This implies classes, likely implementing `View` (custom view components).

Let me think about what's idiomatic. In SwiftUI, you'd have:
```swift
struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            CounterControls(...)
            TextField(...)
            Text(...)
        }
    }
}
```

So `CounterView` and `CounterControls` as `View` implementations is the idiomatic approach. They need to be added to the `permits` list.

Now, how do they get the signals? The cleanest: constructor injection of the signals.

`CounterControls` (the count label + increment button):
```java
public final class CounterControls implements View {
    private final Signal<String> label;
    private final Runnable onIncrement;

    public CounterControls(Signal<String> label, Runnable onIncrement) {
        this.label = label;
        this.onIncrement = onIncrement;
    }

    @Override
    public PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
        return View.hstack(
                View.text(label),
                View.button("Increment", onIncrement)
        ).padding(16).render(env);
    }
}
```

`CounterView` (the root):
```java
public final class CounterView implements View {
    private final View counterControls;
    private final View nameField;
    private final View nameLabel;
    private final View footer;

