One design choice to settle — where the signal is created, which affects how "field-like" the view reads:

Short answer: yes, it's possible — but Java has no property wrappers, so a SwiftUI-`@State`-style annotation that *auto-generates a name* and *auto-connects to the store* must be backed by one of two mechanisms: **runtime reflection** (no codegen) or a **Java annotation processor** (codegen). There's no third way to read a field's name and wrap it transparently.

## Option A — `@Persisted` + reflection (no codegen)

The field is a small `State<T>` holder (not a bare value, since Java can't wrap a field). The framework reflects over the view graph once, reads each field's name, and connects it:

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME) @Target(ElementType.FIELD)
public @interface Persisted { String value() default ""; }   // explicit key, else field name

public final class State<T> {
    private final String name; private final T initial;
    private WritableSignal<T> signal;
    public State(T initial) { ... }
    public T get() { return signal.get(); }
    public void set(T v) { signal.set(v); }
    public void update(UnaryOperator<T> fn) { signal.update(fn); }
}
```

```java
public final class CounterControls implements View {
    @Persisted private final State<Integer> count = new State<>(0);   // name = "count"

    @Override
    public PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
        Signal<String> label = Signals.computed(() -> "Count: " + count.get());
        return View.hstack(View.text(label),
                           View.button("Increment", () -> count.update(v -> v + 1)))
                .padding(16).render(env);
    }
}
```

