## Plan: `body()` as the authoring surface (Option A)

### Summary
Add a SwiftUI-style `View.body() -> View` while keeping the signal-based, diff-emitting emitter. `render(Environment) -> PathlandNode` becomes the *primitive* materializer; composite (app) views declare `body()` and never touch `PathlandNode`. No emitter, transport, renderer, or primitive changes.

### 1. `View.java` (interface)

Replace the single abstract method with a `body()` default + a `render()` default that flattens `body()`:

```java
/**
 * SwiftUI-style composition: a composite view returns its subtree here. Primitives
 * (Text/VStack/Button/…) return {@code this} and instead override
 * {@link #render(Environment)}. Evaluated once at mount (see {@link #render}).
 */
default View body() {
    return this;
}

/**
 * Materialize this view into retained node(s). Composite views resolve {@link #body()}
 * first (recursively); primitives override this to build a {@link PathlandNode}.
 */
default PathlandNode render(Environment env) {
    View resolved = body();
    if (resolved != this) {
        return resolved.render(env);
    }
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(
        getClass().getName() + " must override body() (composite) or render() (primitive)");
}
```

- Update the interface's top javadoc ("build a `PathlandNode` subtree via `render`" → "composite views implement `body()`, primitives implement `render()`").
- All 13 existing primitives (`Text`, `TextField`, `VStack`, `HStack`, `ZStack`, `Spacer`, `Image`, `Rectangle`, `Color`, `Button`, `Modified`, `ButtonStyleView`, `TapGestureView`) keep overriding `render` — **unchanged**.

### 2. Demo views (composite) — `render` → `body`

**`CounterControls.java`**
```java
@Override
public View body() {
    Signal<String> countLabel = Signals.computed(() -> "Count: " + count.get());
    return View.hstack(
            View.text(countLabel),
            View.button("Increment", () -> count.update(v -> v + 1))
    ).padding(16);
}
```

**`NameField.java`**
```java
@Override
public View body() {
    Signal<String> nameLabel = Signals.computed(() -> "Name: " + name.get());
    return View.vstack(
            View.textField("Your name", name.signal()),
            View.text(nameLabel)
    );
}
```

**`CounterView.java`**
```java
@Override
public View body() {
    return View.vstack(
            controls,
            nameField,
            View.text("Pathland · per-session · 16-byte deltas")
                    .color(Color.rgb(0x88, 0x88, 0x88))
    ).padding(24);
}
```

- Drop the trailing `.render(env)`.
- Remove now-unused imports: `com.pathland.view.Environment` and `com.pathland.view.emit.PathlandNode` from all three (keep `View`, `Color`/`Signal`/`Signals`/`State` as used).

### 3. Docs

- `AGENTS.md` (Java DSL section, ~line 223): add a bullet — "`View` has a SwiftUI-style `body()` for composite views and a `render(Environment)` for primitives; `body()` is evaluated once at mount (reactive values come from signals, not body re-evaluation)."

### 4. Verification

- `cd lib/java && JAVA_HOME=… mvn -q clean install` — expect 38 Java tests green (incl. the `EmitterTest` slider anonymous view, which still overrides `render`, and `CounterViewTest`, which mounts `new CounterView()` through the new default `render`).
- Quarkus `quarkus:dev` + Spring Boot jar smoke test: SSR, WS click→`SET_TEXT`, text→`SET_TEXT` deltas, per-session persistence unchanged.

### Notes / non-goals
- **`body()` runs once at mount** — reactivity stays signal-based (`View.text(Signal)`, `Signals.computed`). No re-evaluation.
- **No structural reactivity** — composition is fixed; conditional/`ForEach`-style children remain out of scope (deferred, per the A/B discussion).
- **`PathlandNode` stays in the library** (it's still `render`'s return type and used by `Emitter`), but it drops out of the app-facing surface — the encapsulation win that motivated this.
- Minor tradeoff: a view that overrides *neither* `body()` nor `render()` now fails at runtime (`UnsupportedOperationException`) rather than compile time.

